+/// Whole-range per-instruction hook for the SPR instructions the Unicorn
+/// backend cannot service itself, plus `rfi`.
+///
+/// `rfi` is handled *here*, rather than in a second whole-range code hook, on
+/// purpose. `CodeHook::call` is not told which address it was dispatched for,
+/// so every such hook has to read `pc()` to find its own instruction - and the
+/// `rfi` branch below rewrites `pc()`. Two hooks therefore cannot coexist:
+/// whichever ran second would decode the *return target* instead of the
+/// instruction being dispatched, emulate it, and skip past it from inside the
+/// first hook's callback. Keeping one hook that reads `pc()` once and dispatches
+/// on the opcode makes that unrepresentable rather than merely unlikely.
+///
+/// The branches are mutually exclusive by primary opcode anyway (`rfi` is 19,
+/// `mtspr`/`mfspr`/`mftb` are all 31), but the early return states it outright.
